<%#
  One view, both outcomes. A redirect with a flash message would be the usual
  shape, but flash is stored in the session — and issuing a session cookie is
  the thing these public pages go out of their way not to do.

  subscriber is nil when the honeypot was filled: a bot gets the same page a
  person does, and learns nothing.
%>
<% failed = subscriber&.errors&.any? %>

<% content_for :title, failed ? "That address didn't look right — Remindly" : "You're on the list — Remindly" %>

<% if failed %>
  <h1>That address didn't look right</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    <%= subscriber.errors.full_messages.to_sentence %>. Have another go — or
    just email <a href="mailto:hello@remindly.care">hello@remindly.care</a> and
    I'll add you by hand.
  </p>

  <%= render "shared/subscribe_form", source: "retry" %>
<% else %>
  <h1>You're on the list</h1>

  <%#
    This page must be true in three different situations, only one of which
    sends an email: a genuinely new subscriber, someone already on the list, and
    a bot that filled the honeypot. It used to say the sheet was "on its way to
    your inbox" and to go and check the spam folder — which sent the second
    group hunting for a message that was never sent.

    So it promises nothing about email and hands over the sheet directly, which
    is the thing they actually came for and is public anyway. It also cannot
    tell anyone whether their address was already on the list.
  %>
  <p class="lead">
    Here is the routine sheet —
    <a href="<%= routine_sheet_path %>">open it and print it</a>. One page for
    the fridge door, and you do not have to wait for an email to get hold of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The monthly note will arrive when there is one worth sending: one useful
    thing about caring for a parent at a distance, and a line on what has
    changed in Remindly. Reply to any of them to come off the list, or write to
    <a href="mailto:hello@remindly.care">hello@remindly.care</a> if something
    looks wrong.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= login_path %>">Try Remindly — it's free</a>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="<%= blog_path %>">Read something first</a>
  </div>
<% end %>
